Let $I$ be a finite index set with probability weights $(\pi_i)_{i\in I}$, and let $D_i\ge0$ satisfy $\mathbb E_iD_i=1$. Both $\mathbb E_i$ and $\mathbb E_t$ denote expectation with these weights; in a double expectation the two indices are sampled independently. Fix $0\le x\le1$ and $0<y\le1$, and set $$\Theta=\frac{P(y-x)^2}{y}.$$ Suppose $q_{ti}\in[0,1]$ and $R_i\ge0$ satisfy $$\label{eq:finite-comparison}
 R_i(1-Pq_{ti})\le D_i+D_t\qquad\text{whenever }q_{ti}<1.$$ No symmetry is assumed for $q$. For any $s_i\in[0,1]$, put $r_i=\mathbb E_tq_{ti}$ and $C_i=1-Ps_i$. Then $$\label{eq:finite-inequality}
\begin{aligned}
 \mathbb E_i\Big[&C_i(1-r_i)R_i
 +r_i\{mD_i+(2-s_i)Py(D_i+1)\}-C_i(D_i+1)\\
 &\hspace{12mm}
 -\mathbb E_t(1-q_{ti})\bigl(D_t-(1+m+2Py)D_i\bigr)_+\Big]
 \le2\Theta.
\end{aligned}$$ Here $u_+=\max(u,0)$.

Conditional on the pre-round history, the ideal expression satisfies $$\label{eq:rnd-average-drift}
 \dot f+\mathbb E_{\mathrm{graph}}\mathcal B
 \le BgV+12\zeta V.$$

Let $A_1,\ldots,A_L$ be real symmetric positive semidefinite matrices on a finite-dimensional Euclidean space. There are mutually orthogonal projections $P_1,\ldots,P_L$ such that $\sum_{s=1}^L P_s=I$ and $$\label{eq:orthogonal-splitting}
  \sum_{z=1}^L\sum_{s\ne z}\left\lVert P_sA_zP_s\right\rVert_{\mathrm F}^{2}
  \le C_L\sum_{z\ne z'}\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}(A_zA_{z'}),
  \qquad C_L=2^{L-1}.$$ The sum on the right is over ordered pairs of distinct indices. Zero projections are allowed.

Let $t,r\ge1$ be integers. For each $1\le i\le t$, let $X_i$ and $\mathcal M_i$ be nonempty finite sets with uniform probability, and let $Y_i$ be a specified copy of $(\mathbb F_2)^a$, where $a\ge1$ and $L=2^a$. For $m\in\mathcal M_i$ and $y\in Y_i$, let $\pi_{i,y}^m$ be a probability distribution on $X_i$. Suppose that, for some $\theta\ge0$, these distributions satisfy $$\label{eq:channel-marginal}
  \mathbb E_{m\in\mathcal M_i}\pi_{i,y}^m(x)=\frac1{|X_i|}
  \qquad(x\in X_i, y\in Y_i)$$ and, for every real function $g:X_i\to\mathbb R$ and every $y\in Y_i$, $$\label{eq:channel-variance}
  \mathbb E_{m\in\mathcal M_i}
  \left(\sum_{x\in X_i}\pi_{i,y}^m(x)g(x)-\mathbb E_{x\in X_i}g(x)\right)^2
  \le\theta\mathbb E_{x\in X_i}g(x)^2.$$ Let $F:\prod_iX_i\to\mathbb S_+^r$ satisfy $\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}F(x)\le B$ for all $x$, where $B>0$ and $\mathbb S_+^r$ is the cone of real symmetric positive semidefinite $r$-by-$r$ matrices. Fix $\lambda>1$ such that $$\label{eq:smoothing-parameter}
  L\lambda^2C_L\theta\le1,$$ where $C_L=2^{L-1}$ is the constant of Lemma <a href="#lem:splitting" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:splitting">4</a>. For $m=(m_1,\ldots,m_t)\in\prod_i\mathcal M_i$, define $$\label{eq:product-average}
  \overline F_m(y)
  =\sum_{x\in\prod_iX_i}
     \left(\prod_{i=1}^t\pi_{i,y_i}^{m_i}(x_i)\right)F(x).$$*




*There is a finite integer $R$ and a family of functions $H_q^m:\prod_iY_i\to\mathbb R^{R\times r}$, indexed by $m\in\prod_i\mathcal M_i$ and $q\in\Lambda^t$, such that $$\label{eq:smoothing-squares}
  \overline F_m(y)=\sum_{q\in\Lambda^t}H_q^m(y)^{\mathsf T}H_q^m(y).$$ For each $m,q$, form the horizontal concatenation and its Gram matrix $$\label{eq:smoothing-gram}
  U_q^m=
    \left[\lambda^{\operatorname{dist}(q,p)/2}
           \widehat H_{q,p}^m\right]_{p\in\Lambda^t},
  \qquad C_q^m=U_q^m(U_q^m)^{\mathsf T},$$ where $\widehat H_{q,p}^m$ is the Fourier coefficient of $H_q^m$ at $p$. The family can be chosen so that, for independent uniform $m_i$, $$\label{eq:smoothing-potential}
  \mathbb E_m\sum_{q\in\Lambda^t}\left\lVert C_q^m\right\rVert_{\mathrm F}^2\le2^tB^2.$$ Consequently, at least one $m$ satisfies $$\label{eq:smoothing-coefficients}
  \sum_{q,p\in\Lambda^t}
    \lambda^{\operatorname{dist}(q,p)}\left\lVert\widehat H_{q,p}^m\right\rVert_{\mathrm F}^2
  \le L^t\sqrt r\,B\,2^{t/2}.$$

For every sufficiently large even integer $t$, there is a bipartite $d$-regular multigraph $H$ on $[t]$ such that $$|\delta_H(W)|\geq \beta d\min\{|W|,t-|W|\}
    \qquad (W\subseteq[t]).
    \label{eq:parity-expansion}$$ In particular, $H$ is connected.

Put $a_\ell=\ell(\ell+1)+1$ and $b_\ell=2a_\ell+\ell^2$. For every admissible table, $$\label{smooth:eq-fourth-moment}
  \mathop{\mathrm{tr}}\bigl((T_t^*T_t)^2\bigr)
  \le e^{2a_\ell}(N+1)^{b_\ell}.$$
